const __vite__css = "/* How document elements look - headings, paragraphs, quotes, links, rules,\n   code, list rhythm, tables - kept apart from milkdown-theme.css, which\n   holds what makes the editor WORK (carets, markers, previews, resize\n   handles, table layout).\n\n   All of it sits in a cascade layer. Unlayered CSS beats layered CSS\n   whatever the selectors, so an imported Typora theme's plain\n   `blockquote {}` or `table td {}` now wins over the `.milkdown blockquote`\n   here instead of losing on specificity (#13), and so does the user's own\n   custom CSS. The functional rules stay unlayered on purpose: a theme\n   restyling a list must not be able to break its numbering or the caret.\n\n   Two tiers inside the layer. The plain rules are a neutral base, close to\n   Typora's own base.css, that every theme builds on. The\n   `:not([data-user-theme])` rules are Textus's own ornaments - the h2 rule,\n   the h3 bar, the h4 dot, the tinted quote - which an imported theme never\n   asked for: a theme that styles h3's colour but says nothing of borders\n   would otherwise keep Textus's accent bar. SettingsContext sets\n   data-user-theme while an imported theme is active.\n\n   The layer was `levis-content` before the rename to Textus. It was renamed\n   rather than kept: CSS written for Levis that says\n   `@layer levis-content { ... }` now creates a layer declared after this\n   one, so its rules still override the built-in look (more reliably than\n   before, when specificity inside the shared layer decided), and unlayered\n   CSS wins over both as always (e2e/user-css.spec.ts). */\n\n@layer textus-content {\n  /* The document's type. The size follows the column: the classic 16.5px\n     at the 760px standard measure, growing to 18px as the adaptive column\n     widens (app/editor-column.css --content-width), so a wide column doesn't read as\n     small print. cqi is the column's zoomed width, hence the divide; rem,\n     so an `html { font-size }` scales